1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,834
It can't understand our diagrams

2
00:00:03,134 --> 00:00:05,439
or our images, or our videos.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:08,742
Two architecture diagrams.

4
00:00:09,042 --> 00:00:13,672
In the first, service A calls service B. In the second, B calls A.

5
00:00:13,972 --> 00:00:18,453
That arrow is the whole meaning, and to most pipelines the two look the same.

6
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:26,521
In the research track, Semantic Codex indexes images and sampled video frames with a pixel hash.

7
00:00:26,821 --> 00:00:33,499
Compare the diagram with its reversed copy: distance zero. Identical appearance, opposite meaning.

8
00:00:33,799 --> 00:00:38,429
A different diagram scores thirteen, and an unrelated image thirty-five.

9
00:00:38,729 --> 00:00:43,487
Every result says identity unknown, and admission stays inconclusive.

10
00:00:44,833 --> 00:00:46,541
Appearance is not meaning.

11
00:00:46,841 --> 00:01:00,516
Video frames are sampled with time anchors, up to twelve from the first minute. An optional scene search ranks frames against a text prompt, but its scores are uncalibrated, and it never identifies people.

12
00:01:01,867 --> 00:01:03,169
That's the boundary.

13
00:01:03,469 --> 00:01:15,288
QualityRag can find and anchor visual media. It doesn't read diagrams, extract text from images, or recognise people. Visual evidence never admits on its own.

14
00:01:15,588 --> 00:01:18,383
Next: the right rule, for the wrong customer.
